"""Step 5 teacher specifications. Finished student packets belong to Step 6."""
PROMPTS = [
"Order 2400 BCE, 1800 BCE and 500 CE. Explain circa. Classify an excavated tablet and a modern museum caption. Which two source types could answer how a settlement obtained food, and how would they offer different perspectives?",
"Use both site cases to explain one continuity and two changes between mobile and settled lives. Connect domestication, surplus and specialization; explain why a failed harvest weakens an automatic-surplus claim.",
"Label Tigris, Euphrates, Zagros, Mediterranean and Persian Gulf. Recommend one of the two proposed settlement locations using two mapped features, a risk and an adaptation that changes the environment.",
"Annotate all seven civilization characteristics on your city diagram. Explain how irrigation and one transport technology affect specialization and trade; identify the institution organizing work and a possible disruption.",
"Compare city-state, monarchy and empire. Explain how social position affects daily life; use a tablet, ziggurat or epic to connect belief and institution. Whose perspective is preserved and whose is missing?",
"Compare Hammurabi laws 8 and 48 with the artifact/context account. What purpose and audience can you infer? Explain why written rules do not prove equal treatment or actual enforcement.",
"Write a 150–250-word or equivalent reconstruction argument using a map and artifact/text. Explain two interacting causes of city development, consider an alternative and identify a source limitation. Revise one causal link after feedback.",
"Using the held-back artifact/caption, record an observation, a contextual inference and a limit. Explain whether it strengthens or weakens a link on your city board; justify the change independently.",
"Label all seven Egyptian map features from the teacher key. Explain flood/calendar/irrigation decisions and one occupational relationship; attribute an afterlife belief without claiming the belief is independently proven.",
"Distinguish Hatshepsut's policy, Ramses II's conquest and the modern discovery of Tutankhamun. Explain hieroglyphs, papyrus and Giza's significance. Use one Nubian-perspective clue to qualify a trade/conflict claim.",
"Locate the six Israel features. Attribute the traditional Ur–Canaan–Egypt account. Complete a Judaism profile naming Abraham, Moses, Tanakh/Torah, monotheism, commandments and ethical responsibility; distinguish tradition from corroborated evidence.",
"Sequence Saul, David, Solomon, Assyrian/Babylonian conquest, exile and Persian return. Explain political causes and compare one movement with Egypt/Nubia using a similarity, a difference and dated evidence.",
"Label India's six features and explain monsoon effects. Use the Indus plan to explain grid, bricks and sanitation. Compare with Mesopotamia and state what a street plan cannot establish about rulers or religion.",
"Compare Hinduism and Buddhism using people/texts, goals and beliefs. Explain Aryan migration/language and hierarchy with a bounded daily-life example. Date medicine/yoga/numerals separately from Indus urbanism; do not assign all achievements to one era.",
"Compare two societies using geography and institutions, citing one source each. Answer the teacher's third-society question. Show one evidence-based correction in the three-panel atlas and explain its importance.",
"Explain one source for each atlas society. Interpret a fresh dated map/context card: locate its setting, make a supported claim and identify a limitation. Revise one caption; keep your private response separate from the team atlas.",
"Label China's six features and sequence Xia tradition, Shang evidence and Zhou authority. Explain one geographic influence without claiming complete isolation. Contrast Mandate of Heaven and Legalism as governing ideas.",
"Explain Qin unification/standardization and Confucius/Analects/kinship/order; connect a Han institution to Confucian ideas. Classify paper, silk, seismograph, compass, porcelain and printing claims by evidence/date; reject unsupported Han printing attribution.",
"Label seven Greek features. Compare Athens and Sparta in government, education and eligible participation, including women and enslaved people. Explain a mountain/sea influence and how timed speaking can shape participation.",
"Distinguish Persian and Peloponnesian conflicts and their consequences. Connect Socrates, Plato and Aristotle to different questions. Explain Zeus/Olympics and columns/Parthenon/Acropolis; date Alexander and Hellenistic diffusion and qualify an astronomical-mechanism claim.",
"Write a comparison plan for Chinese and Greek timekeeping evidence. Specify elapsed-time question, seconds, fixed vessel/hole/start level and three repeats. Explain how supplied shadow observations differ from your experiment and what neither can prove about ancient accuracy.",
"Report the three trial times with units and range. Explain one reason variation might occur and one shadow-table constraint. Use one source from each society to distinguish documented historical use from your modern model's result.",
"Annotate Silk Road and Hellenistic exchange with dates/intermediaries, including Buddhism's spread and Alexander's context. Explain one exchange mechanism and why similar inventions alone cannot prove direct China–Greece borrowing.",
"Explain a Chinese and a Greek achievement with institutional context and a source limit. Interpret a fresh dated invention record and your timer data. Revise the comparison without treating classroom results as a ranking of civilizations.",
"Label Rome's six features, dating Constantinople as a later capital. Diagram republic branches/checks and Twelve Tables; explain patrician/plebeian/enslaved participation and a geographic mechanism of expansion.",
"Sequence Caesar's military/popular power, dictatorship/assassination and Augustus/Pax Romana. Explain two interacting causes of institutional change and one continuity. Infer a ruler claim's audience/purpose using a contrasting account.",
"Explain two infrastructure systems using flow/use labels; identify functions of arches, aqueducts, bridges, domes, roads, sanitation and Colosseum. Connect polytheistic civic use and explain unequal benefits or costs.",
"Complete a Christianity profile naming Jesus, Paul, Bible, monotheism, forgiveness, eternal life and Messiah as attributed beliefs. Explain a dated Jewish diaspora phase using political conflict and movement evidence; avoid one universal departure narrative.",
"Compare dated East/West maps, division and Constantine's capital. Explain two governance/location factors, one eastern continuity and why western collapse does not mean all Roman institutions ended.",
"Rank two influences on western collapse using evidence and mechanisms; incorporate a third interacting cause, a competing explanation and a source limit. Explain why invasion alone or unsupported moral decline is insufficient.",
"Write an independent 200–300-word or equivalent account linking republic-to-empire change and western collapse. Use multiple sources, distinguish eastern continuation and address a competing explanation; revise reasoning after source/sequence critique.",
"Respond privately to a held-back historical account/map: identify claim, context, credibility and a limit. Explain whether it changes your causal account. Support one council response with evidence and record your own revision.",
]
EXAMPLES = [
"On different dates, 3000 BCE precedes 1000 BCE; circa signals approximation. An object is primary evidence for its manufacture, while a present caption is secondary interpretation; neither alone establishes every user's beliefs.",
"A hypothetical settlement has grain storage and weaving tools: storage may support specialized work, but flood damage could interrupt it. Tools remain in both cases; settlement does not imply all mobile communities vanished.",
"A hypothetical riverbank provides water but exposes fields to floods; a canal is a human adaptation with labor and maintenance costs. A map's date and purpose limit boundary claims.",
"A teacher-created canal diagram links organized maintenance to water delivery, yield and exchanged surplus. A broken channel interrupts the mechanism; drawing a wheel without explaining transport provides no causal evidence.",
"A hypothetical royal inscription praises a ruler: its audience/purpose may explain emphasis on victories. A worker's experience remains unknown; an epic can reveal ideals without proving every narrated event.",
"Law 48 addresses harvest failure and debt obligations. A written rule supports a claim about prescribed responses, while enforcement needs other evidence. Compare a different rule before inferring equal treatment.",
"Use a different settlement case: map access plus storage evidence support an exchange explanation, but stored goods alone do not identify ownership. A competing seasonal-storage interpretation narrows the conclusion.",
"A held-back tool's worn edge supports use but not the user's social rank. Add a dated caption before drawing a broader city-life inference.",
"Upper Egypt lies upstream toward the south; river direction and north are different concepts. Calendar knowledge helps plan work but cannot guarantee harvests; occupations depend on institutions as well as the river.",
"A monument records an ancient ruler's claim; a modern discovery record dates recovery. Neither dates the other's event. A trade object supports contact without proving peaceful relations in every period.",
"A religious narrative supports what a community preserves about origins; a modern historical map is an interpretation. Attribute a movement account and seek independent evidence before asserting a precise route.",
"Compare a different conquest sequence: conquest can change rulers, cause displacement and later permit returns under another empire. Return does not mean every displaced family returned.",
"A hypothetical city plan shows drains and regular streets. These support organized construction, but cannot name its rulers or prove all houses had equal access.",
"A matched profile separates Hindu moksha and Buddhist nirvana without treating them as identical. A later numeral record illustrates chronological separation from an earlier city plan.",
"Compare Nile irrigation and Indus drainage through purpose, evidence and limits. Both manage water; their distinct environmental problems prevent a single identical explanation.",
"A new regional map locates contact, but lacks a trade object's maker or journey. Revise an overconfident route caption to an evidenced possibility.",
"Shang material records and Xia traditions offer different evidence conditions. Mountains can constrain routes while exchange still occurs; geography influences possibilities without dictating one government.",
"A dated later printing example cannot automatically be Han because it is Chinese. Distinguish a earliest surviving record from an invention date; a text praising order does not prove universal social agreement.",
"Timed courtroom speaking can regulate participation, yet eligibility still excludes many people. Compare who could speak and who could hold power before applying the word democracy.",
"A later Hellenistic mechanism predicts celestial cycles; this supports a bounded calculation claim. It does not prove ordinary ships navigated with it or every Greek possessed one.",
"Illustrative repeats of 32, 35 and 33 seconds have a 3-second range. Fixing start level helps comparison; a replica cannot independently establish an ancient device's accuracy.",
"For illustrative values 32,35,33 seconds, range=35−32=3 seconds. A changing water head may affect flow. One successful repeat still leaves construction and historical-use uncertainty.",
"A route with intermediaries can carry goods or ideas without a single direct journey. Similar shapes are weaker transmission evidence than dated contact plus shared technical features.",
"A fresh record dated 868 CE qualifies a Han printing caption. Use chronology to revise the attribution, while recognizing a surviving print does not itself prove the first invention date.",
"An institutional diagram shows a check on officials but also eligibility limits. A road/marine route may help movement; access alone does not explain every conquest.",
"A ruler's public achievement account emphasizes success; compare a separate account before explaining transition. Caesar's dictatorship and Augustus's principate belong at different timeline points.",
"An aqueduct moves water using a gradient; distribution depends on maintenance and decisions. A monumental arch shows construction technique without establishing equal household access.",
"A sacred account expresses a faith claim; historical spread uses dated movement and institution evidence. A diaspora map should distinguish separate conflicts and continuing communities.",
"A dated eastern capital map preserves Roman administration after western political collapse. Changing boundaries and ongoing institutions can occur together.",
"Military pressure and revenue loss may reinforce one another. Ranking a cause requires explaining its reach and interaction, rather than declaring it more interesting.",
"An account that says invasions caused everything omits institutional and economic mechanisms. Add a sourced interaction and eastern contrast instead of a longer list of unsupported causes.",
"A fresh account can strengthen one causal mechanism while leaving another uncertain. Civil critique asks what evidence would change a claim and avoids attacking its author personally.",
]
SECTIONS = [
"3.1 Early Civilizations: evidence/timeline introduction", "2.3 The Neolithic Revolution: Development of Agriculture and How Farming Changed Human Experience",
"3.2 Ancient Mesopotamia: geography and settlement", "3.2 Ancient Mesopotamia: agriculture, city-states and technologies",
"3.2 Ancient Mesopotamia: institutions, religion and writing", "3.2 Ancient Mesopotamia: Hammurabi; paired numbered laws 8 and 48 in Fordham translation",
"Reuse H1 source log; no new long reading", "Held-back artifact/caption; reuse H1 source log",
"3.3 Ancient Egypt: geography, agriculture, religion and society", "3.3 Ancient Egypt: rulers and material culture; paired Egypt/Nubia contexts",
"4.2 The Hebrews: origin traditions and Judaism", "4.2 The Hebrews: kingdoms, conquest, exile and return",
"5.4 Vedic India to the Fall of the Maurya Empire: Indus cities and geographic context", "5.4 Vedic India to the Fall of the Maurya Empire: Vedic society, Hinduism and Buddhism",
"Reuse three H2 society profiles", "Held-back map/context; reuse three profiles",
"5.1 Ancient China: early dynasties, geography and Zhou authority", "5.1 Ancient China: Qin/Han and Confucian institutions; Met Han essay, government and timekeeping paragraphs",
"6.1 Early Greek Culture and Politics: geography and polis comparison", "6.2 Ancient Greece: wars and classical ideas; 6.3 The Hellenistic Era: Alexander and diffusion",
"MacTutor Water-clocks: opening through Athenian courts and flow/pressure explanation; Met Han timekeeping paragraph", "Reread selected clock contexts; teacher-created shadow table and recorded class observations",
"5.1 Ancient China: Silk Road; 6.3 The Hellenistic Era: diffusion", "Reuse H3 contexts; held-back dated printing record",
"6.4 The Roman Republic: institutions, law, social groups and geography", "6.4 The Roman Republic: late republic; 6.5 The Age of Augustus: transition and Pax Romana",
"7.1 The Daily Life of a Roman Family and 7.3 The Roman Economy: selected infrastructure/daily-life passages", "7.4 Religion in the Roman Empire: religious beliefs, Christianity and Jewish communities",
"10.1 The Eastward Shift: Constantine, regional division and eastern continuation", "10.1 The Eastward Shift: pressures and western collapse; contrast interpretations within the account",
"Reuse H4 dossier and dated maps", "Held-back account/map; reuse H4 dossier",
]

def enrich(projects, resources, specs, tn):
    for i,s in enumerate(SECTIONS):
        SECTIONS[i]=s.replace('4.2 The Hebrews','4.4 The Hebrews').replace('6.1 Early Greek Culture and Politics','6.2 Ancient Greece')
    clock=next(r for r in resources if r['id']=='CLOCK')
    clock.update(title='University of St Andrews MacTutor — Timekeeping in the Ancient World: Water-clocks',url='https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Water_clocks/',selection='Daniel Mintz, April 2007. Opening through Athenian judicial use; flow/pressure explanation. Pair with Met Han timekeeping interpretation. Modern scholarly account, not an ancient eyewitness.',status='Teacher text reviewed; accessible student adaptation remains Step 6. Archaeological primary object selection remains conditional.')
    resources.append(dict(id='LAW',title='Fordham Ancient History Sourcebook — Code of Hammurabi, L. W. King translation',url='https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/ancient/hamcode.asp',selection='Numbered laws 8 and 48 only; original eighteenth century BCE, modern English translation. Do not use the dated introductory commentary as the factual key.',status='Translation selected. Prepare short attributed accessible versions and pair with object/context evidence in Step 6.'))
    projects[0]['resources'].append('LAW')
    chapters={'2.3':'2-3-the-neolithic-revolution','3.1':'3-1-early-civilizations','3.2':'3-2-ancient-mesopotamia','3.3':'3-3-ancient-egypt','4.4':'4-4-the-hebrews','5.1':'5-1-ancient-china','5.4':'5-4-vedic-india-to-the-fall-of-the-maurya-empire','6.2':'6-2-ancient-greece','6.3':'6-3-the-hellenistic-era','6.4':'6-4-the-roman-republic','6.5':'6-5-the-age-of-augustus','7.1':'7-1-the-daily-life-of-a-roman-family','7.3':'7-3-the-roman-economy-trade-taxes-and-conquest','7.4':'7-4-religion-in-the-roman-empire','10.1':'10-1-the-eastward-shift'}
    import re
    for r in resources:
        if r['id']=='HISTORY':r['status']='Named section selections specified in all 32 detailed plans. Bounded Grade 6 adaptations and source boxes remain Step 6; do not assign whole college chapters.'
    all_lessons=[l for p in projects for l in p['lessons']]
    for i,l in enumerate(l for p in projects for l in p['lessons']):
        l['prompt']=PROMPTS[i];l['worked_example']=EXAMPLES[i];l['source_selection']=SECTIONS[i]
        l['section_links']=[dict(title='OpenStax '+key,url='https://openstax.org/books/world-history-volume-1/pages/'+chapters[key]) for key in dict.fromkeys(re.findall(r'\b\d+\.\d+\b',SECTIONS[i])) if key in chapters]
        l['outcomes']=[l['evidence'], 'Explain relevant evidence and context while identifying a limitation.']
        l['reading']='In class: 150–250 words of attributed teacher adaptation from '+SECTIONS[i]+', with at most two map/image/source cards. Read once for context and reread the cited clue; preview 3–5 topic terms. Adaptations are proposed materials, not finished packets.'
        l['metadata']='OpenStax, World History Volume 1 (2023), modern secondary synthesis from Rice University: teaching audience, context/explanation purpose. For every reproduced primary item retain original creator or unknown, approximate date, find/origin place, intended audience/purpose when evidenced, collection/translation and stable source. Unknown metadata stays unknown; do not invent it.'
        l['developing']='Developing response: “'+l['title']+' proves everyone lived the same way.” Feedback: identify the time/place and one concrete clue, explain the mechanism, then name a person/group or claim the source cannot establish. Recheck with the different case specified below.'
        l['steps']=[
          'Retrieve starting work: '+l['prereq']+' Ask learners to show one prior claim and its evidence; use gaps to select the short support clinic.',
          'Read the bounded selection and inspect metadata. Learners mark one observation, one interpretation and an unfamiliar term. Teacher supplies topic context from: '+l['content'],
          'Demonstrate: '+l['model']+' Think aloud using the different-content worked example below; learners explain why the inference is bounded before trying their own clue.',
          'Build from saved work: '+l['activity']+' Assign recorder, evidence checker, questioner and organizer in group blocks, rotating next block; everyone keeps a private reasoning note. In individual blocks work independently after the guided turn.',
          'Collect the private response to the exact assessment prompt below. Allow source log and vocabulary aids, but no team-composed answers; record oral/text access separately. Ask a follow-up when a named component is missing.',
          'Feedback and revision: '+l['support']+' Use the developing response to contrast assertion with supported reasoning. Save the original plus correction, label unresolved learning, and carry it to the next block clinic.']
        l['student_directions']='Start with your saved notes. Read the assigned source cards and mark the clue you use. '+l['activity'].split(' Compare a brief')[0]+' Explain your own answer to the teacher prompt. Show one change you made after feedback and why.'
        l['assessment_conditions']='Practice is coached. The private check is completed without a teammate supplying reasoning, with the assigned source log available. Equivalent oral, dictated or typed responses preserve the same history/evidence demands. Teacher records support and unanswered components separately; unobserved learning is not mastery.'
        l['grading']='Secure for the specified task: '+l['criteria']+' Developing: relevant facts/clues present but a required explanation, date, comparison or limitation is missing; give component feedback. Needs further evidence: unsupported/inaccurate account or no independent response; reteach and collect a fresh check. Group accuracy/source attribution and organization receive separate descriptive feedback, not an individual achievement substitute.'
        l['recheck']='Use a different dated '+('map with a changed boundary/feature' if any(x in l['title'].lower() for x in ['locate','map','trace','nile']) else 'source card with one unfamiliar claim and full attribution')+'. Ask which part of the previous conclusion still holds, what the new clue supports and what remains unknown. Collect a fresh explanation after the clinic, without copying the worked example.'
        l['extension']='Compare a plausible alternative interpretation using one existing source. Identify what additional evidence would discriminate between it and the current claim; do not add another polished product.'
        l['materials']='Prepare the bounded source adaptation, map/image cards with captions/text equivalents, vocabulary strip, private response prompt, model and developing-response display, and held-back recheck case. Reuse the project board/atlas/display/dossier and source log; provide large-print/digital equivalents. Finished copies and keys remain Step 6.'
        # Busy context blocks receive extra reading/modeling; final group events give six teams time.
        if l['week']==4 and l['mode']=='Group':
            l['agenda']=[(6,'Retrieve evidence and explain discussion criteria'),(8,'Model bounded critique and inspect fresh source'),(36,'Project walkthroughs: six teams × six minutes, four speakers each; teacher records one 45-second explanation per learner'),(15,'Independent fresh-source response'),(15,'Project revision using feedback'),(7,'Targeted support and different-case recheck'),(3,'Archive and transition')]
        elif i in [13,19]:
            l['agenda']=[(5,'Retrieval and vocabulary'),(15,'Two bounded context readings/cards'),(18,'Explicit instruction, worked model and guided turn'),(32,'Project comparison and saved annotations'),(10,'Private component check'),(7,'Feedback, support and different-case recheck'),(3,'Save and transition')]
        else:
            l['agenda']=[(7,'Retrieve prior evidence'),(11,'Read bounded sources with context support'),(17,'Explicit instruction, worked model and guided turn'),(35,'Project analysis and creation from saved work'),(10,'Private evidence check'),(7,'Feedback, support and different-case recheck'),(3,'Save and transition')]
        l['agenda']=[dict(minutes=m,task=t) for m,t in l['agenda']]
        l['model_summary']=l['model']+' Example: '+l['worked_example']
        l['activity_summary']='1. Read/mark evidence. 2. Guided source turn. 3. '+l['activity'].split(' Compare a brief')[0]+' 4. Private check. 5. Revise and save. Full procedures and clock in linked plan.'
        l['check_summary']='Prompt: '+l['prompt']+'\nCriteria: '+l['criteria']+'\nResponse to difficulty: '+l['support']
        l['skills']='Teach / revisit: '+l['title']+'\nPractice: source/context reasoning through the project task.\nCheck: '+l['evidence']+'\nBuilds on: '+l['prereq']
        nxt=all_lessons[i+1]['id'] if i+1<len(all_lessons) else 'the next history course'
        l['handoff']+=' Carry saved private evidence and unresolved component notes to '+nxt+'; use its opening retrieval/clinic budget for a fresh check. Final unresolved needs go in the receiving teacher record. No homework required.'
        l['verification']='Specified in Step 5: explicit model/review, project practice and the private prompt above support the listed selected contributions. Materials and observed attainment remain unverified. Topic-dependent source claims are conditional until the attributed packet is checked.'
        l['standards_routes']=[dict(id=k,role='Primary',teach=specs[k],practice=l['activity'],check=l['prompt'],criterion=l['criteria'],status='Specified; packet-dependent claims Conditional') for k in l['primary']]+[dict(id=k,role='Supporting',teach=specs[k],practice='Gather and corroborate source clues in the project log.',check='Cumulative source log, not separately certified whole-standard attainment.',criterion='Attributed, relevant clues; uncertainties recorded.',status='Supporting practice specified') for k in l['supporting']]
    law=projects[0]['lessons'][5];law['resources'].append('LAW')
    oral=projects[1]['lessons'][1]
    oral['source_condition']='Modern archaeology oral account remains to select and verify (60–90 seconds, attributable speaker/date/context, transcript). Use the written discovery record now; it cannot satisfy the oral modality. SSP.01 oral contribution stays Conditional.'
    oral['activity']=oral['activity'].split(' Compare a brief')[0]
    oral['evidence']=oral['evidence'].replace(' Include one comparison of the oral recollection and discovery record.',' Oral/written comparison is collected only after the oral source is verified; otherwise this modality remains Conditional.')
    oral['steps'][3]=oral['steps'][3].split(' Compare a brief')[0]+' If the oral source has been verified, compare its recollection to the written record; otherwise omit that comparison and retain the conditional flag.'
    timer=projects[2]['lessons'][5]
    timer['agenda']=[dict(minutes=m,task=t) for m,t in [(5,'Recall the comparison plan'),(10,'Inspect clock contexts and demonstrate consistent start'),(8,'Dry-run roles and read labels'),(30,'Project investigation: three short trials, record seconds, reset start level; analyze range and supplied shadow table'),(12,'Project comparison caption and historical limit'),(10,'Private data/source interpretation'),(8,'Feedback and different-data recheck'),(7,'Empty trays, dry area, save records')]]
    timer['materials']+=' Six adult-prepared plastic vessels with identical marked start/stop levels and pre-punched holes, six catch trays, measuring jugs, water, towels, stopwatches and recording cards; no student cutting. Pilot drain interval 30–60 seconds before class. If water access is unavailable, supply explicitly labeled demonstration data and record that direct investigation was not observed.'
    timer['steps'][3]='Teams set vessels over trays, fill to the marked start line, uncover hole and start timing together; stop at the second mark. Record seconds and irregularities, refill to identical level and repeat three times. Rotate timer, reader, recorder and reset/observer roles so everyone contributes. Calculate max minus min; compare supplied shadow constraints. Draft the same display caption, not a second product.'
    timer['recheck']='Use a different illustrative dataset: 41,39,40 seconds. Calculate its 2-second range; explain why variation does not establish ancient accuracy. If procedure was inconsistent, repeat one correctly reset demonstration and collect an independent explanation.'
    timer['activity_summary']='Teacher demonstrates/reset check; teams record three consistent-start trials, analyze range, compare supplied shadows, draft a bounded historical caption. Collect private interpretation; clean up. Full procedure in linked plan.'
    for t in tn:
        if t['id']=='SSP.01':t['status']='Conditional';t['action']='Written/artifact/map modalities specified. Select and verify the modern oral archaeology account before claiming the oral modality; written recollection alone is insufficient.'
        elif t['id']!='6.39':t['action']='Detailed teaching and private assessment specification exists at named routes. Produce/check source packets and response keys, then observe individual components; route coverage is not demonstrated mastery.'
